            <ArticleTitle>Perioperative challenges in management of a deaf and dumb patient posted for high-risk cardiac surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>564-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/0971-9784.185567</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Perioperative management of deaf and dumb patients can be a challenging task. For smooth postoperative recovery, proper care should begin in the preoperative period. Understanding the patients' needs and training him to follow the instructions requires to involve a communication specialist. Judicious use of sedatives and analgesics is essential to keep the patient pain-free and comfortable. Postoperatively, the patient should be kept awake, enough to understand the internal need of the body and to make a meaningful response to external stimuli. Adequate preoperative planning and coordinated team efforts with involvement of specialists can help in delivering better postoperative care.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
